  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "yr_re_ast_split_at_chaining_point",
    "file": "yara/libyara/re.c",
    "description": "The AST split routine incorrectly unlinks and frees a chaining-point node when that node is the head of the root CONCAT. It updates `children_tail` and neighboring sibling links but never updates `re_ast->root_node->children_head`. For this PoV the chaining node is the sole child, leaving `children_head` pointing to freed memory while `children_tail` becomes NULL. The function returns success; later `yr_parser_reduce_string_declaration` calls `yr_re_ast_destroy(re_ast)` at parser.c:668, and `yr_re_node_destroy` traverses the stale `children_head`, causing the heap-use-after-free.",
    "conditions": "A parsed regex AST must have a CONCAT whose first child is an ungreedy `RE_NODE_RANGE_ANY` with a bound above `YR_STRING_CHAINING_THRESHOLD`. The input `rule r{strings:$=/.{,}?/` creates a sole CONCAT child for `.{,}?`: omitted bounds produce start 0/end 32767, `?` makes it ungreedy, and the large end triggers splitting. With no previous or next sibling, the split leaves the root head dangling after freeing the child. The parser then successfully continues string reduction and destroys the malformed original AST, dereferencing the freed node; the missing closing rule brace is not material to the crash."
  },
